1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,230
This hot dog and soda have cost a dollar fifty since nineteen eighty-five.

2
00:00:05,549 --> 00:00:06,780
Forty years.

3
00:00:07,099 --> 00:00:13,630
Same price. And the company selling it couldn't care less because it's not where the money comes from.

4
00:00:14,279 --> 00:00:18,550
Costco sells everything, from rotisserie chickens to kayaks.

5
00:00:18,870 --> 00:00:22,300
But selling stuff isn't really how it makes its money.

6
00:00:22,620 --> 00:00:27,570
By the end of this video, you'll see the hidden gear that powers the whole warehouse.

7
00:00:28,219 --> 00:00:36,759
Meet Penny. Before she can buy a single thing, she has to pay Costco sixty-five dollars a year, just to walk through the door.

8
00:00:37,079 --> 00:00:39,979
It feels like a cover charge for groceries.

9
00:00:40,299 --> 00:00:43,049
And that cover charge is the whole trick.

10
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:45,240
Penny isn't alone.

11
00:00:45,560 --> 00:00:48,539
Costco has eighty-one million paid members.

12
00:00:48,859 --> 00:00:53,880
Last year, their fees added up to more than five point three billion dollars.

13
00:00:54,530 --> 00:00:58,509
Now compare that to Costco's entire profit for the year:

14
00:00:58,829 --> 00:01:00,899
about eight point one billion.

15
00:01:01,219 --> 00:01:05,299
The membership fees alone are almost two-thirds the size of it.

16
00:01:05,620 --> 00:01:09,030
And fees cost Costco almost nothing to collect.

17
00:01:09,349 --> 00:01:11,140
Think about what that means.

18
00:01:11,459 --> 00:01:21,340
Before Costco sells a single hot dog, it has already collected billions of dollars, paid up front, from people who are basically promising to come back.

19
00:01:21,659 --> 00:01:28,340
That money is so dependable that Costco can afford to sell everything else on razor-thin margins.

20
00:01:28,989 --> 00:01:31,340
Here's the best part, for Costco.

21
00:01:31,659 --> 00:01:37,439
In the US and Canada, more than ninety-two percent of members renew every year.

22
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:39,209
That's not a store.

23
00:01:39,530 --> 00:01:41,000
That's a subscription.

24
00:01:41,650 --> 00:01:43,950
So why keep the hot dog so cheap?

25
00:01:44,269 --> 00:01:45,620
Because it's bait.

26
00:01:45,939 --> 00:01:53,489
Costco's own leaders have said the price isn't changing, even though inflation should have pushed it well past four dollars.

27
00:01:54,140 --> 00:01:57,640
Same with the four ninety-nine rotisserie chicken.

28
00:01:57,959 --> 00:02:01,900
Costco keeps famous prices like these low on purpose.

29
00:02:02,219 --> 00:02:05,299
They make the membership feel like a no-brainer.

30
00:02:05,620 --> 00:02:13,060
And on her way to grab a cheap chicken, Penny somehow picks up a TV, forty rolls of paper towels, and a kayak.

31
00:02:13,710 --> 00:02:15,349
Let's do Penny's math.

32
00:02:15,669 --> 00:02:18,189
She paid sixty-five dollars to get in.

33
00:02:18,509 --> 00:02:25,849
If Costco saves her ten percent, she has to spend six hundred and fifty dollars a year just to break even.

34
00:02:26,169 --> 00:02:33,229
But divide Costco's total sales by its paid members, and it comes to more than three thousand dollars each.

35
00:02:33,550 --> 00:02:35,919
Members aren't just breaking even.

36
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:38,819
They're moving their whole shopping life in.

37
00:02:39,469 --> 00:02:41,159
Then comes the upgrade.

38
00:02:41,479 --> 00:02:50,120
The executive membership costs a hundred and thirty dollars, double the basic fee, and gives some of it back as rewards on what you spend.

39
00:02:50,439 --> 00:02:53,250
The more you spend, the better the deal looks.

40
00:02:53,569 --> 00:02:59,310
And the only way to make the upgrade pay off is to bring even more of your shopping to Costco.

41
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:03,270
Almost thirty-nine million members have taken it.

42
00:03:03,590 --> 00:03:09,500
And those executive members account for nearly three quarters of everything Costco sells.

43
00:03:10,150 --> 00:03:11,849
So here's the machine.

44
00:03:12,169 --> 00:03:16,379
Costco sells products at thin margins to keep prices low.

45
00:03:16,699 --> 00:03:19,770
Low prices make the membership feel worth it.

46
00:03:20,090 --> 00:03:22,479
Members renew, year after year.

47
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:25,909
And those renewals are where the real money is.

48
00:03:26,229 --> 00:03:29,580
The warehouse is just the showroom for the subscription.

49
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:32,949
Costco doesn't need you to buy any one thing.

50
00:03:33,270 --> 00:03:35,729
It needs you to come back next year.

51
00:03:36,379 --> 00:03:38,449
So, is a membership worth it?

52
00:03:38,770 --> 00:03:41,449
It can be. But do the math.

53
00:03:41,770 --> 00:03:46,340
One: only count savings on things you'd buy anyway.

54
00:03:46,659 --> 00:03:51,330
Two: bulk is only cheaper if you use it before it goes bad.

55
00:03:51,650 --> 00:03:56,810
Three: if the upgrade doesn't pay for itself in rewards, stay basic.

56
00:03:57,460 --> 00:03:59,430
Pawn shops sell you time.

57
00:03:59,750 --> 00:04:01,919
Costco sells you belonging.

58
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:06,080
But there's one business that sells you something even stranger:

59
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:08,389
the feeling that you might win.

60
00:04:08,710 --> 00:04:12,060
How casinos make money, next on Money Mechanics.

